---
import '../styles/global.css';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Navigation from '../components/Navigation.astro';
import BackButton from '../components/BackButton.astro';
import BreathingExercise from '../components/BreathingExercise.tsx';
---

<BaseLayout title="Guided Breathing" description="Breathe deeply with visual guidance. Choose from multiple calming breathing patterns to help you relax and sleep.">
  <div class="page">
    <div class="page__header">
      <BackButton />
      <h1 class="page__title">Guided Breathing</h1>
      <p class="page__desc">Breathe deeply, sleep soundly</p>
    </div>
    <BreathingExercise client:load />
  </div>

  <article class="article-guide">
    <div class="article-guide__inner">
      <h2>The Physiology of Breathing for Deep Sleep</h2>
      <p>
        Breathing is the only autonomic bodily function that humans can consciously control. While your digestion, immune function, and endocrine system operate without deliberate effort, your respiration provides a direct manual override to your nervous system. By consciously altering the tempo and depth of your breath, you can signal safety directly to your brainstem.
      </p>
      <p>
        When stress, racing thoughts, or physical exhaustion keep you awake, your sympathetic nervous system ("fight or flight") is overactive. Rhythmic, slow-paced breathing stimulates the <strong>Vagus Nerve</strong>, the primary cranial nerve governing the parasympathetic nervous system ("rest and digest"). This rapid biological shift lowers arterial blood pressure, slows your resting pulse, and primes the brain for restorative sleep.
      </p>

      <div class="guide-callout">
        <strong>The Exhalation Key:</strong> Inhaling temporarily accelerates your heart rate by compressing the thoracic cavity. Prolonging your exhalation triggers acetylcholine release via the vagus nerve, immediately decelerating cardiac output.
      </div>

      <h3>Three Clinically Backed Breathing Protocols</h3>
      <div class="guide-card-grid">
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>1. 4-7-8 Relaxing Breath</h4>
          <p>Inhale for 4 seconds, hold gently for 7 seconds, and exhale steadily through the mouth for 8 seconds. Popularized by Dr. Andrew Weil, this pattern acts as a natural neurological tranquilizer.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>2. Box Breathing (4-4-4-4)</h4>
          <p>Equal parts inhale, hold, exhale, and hold for 4 seconds each. Developed for high-stress military tactical operations, it rapidly balances carbon dioxide levels and settles mental agitation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="guide-card">
          <h4>3. Coherent Breathing</h4>
          <p>A gentle, continuous 5-second inhale followed by a 5-second exhale (approximately 6 breaths per minute). This rhythm optimizes Heart Rate Variability (HRV) for bedtime recovery.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3>How to Practice Guided Breathing for Sleep</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Position your body:</strong> Lie flat on your back with a pillow supporting your neck. Place one hand gently on your belly and the other on your upper chest to encourage diaphragmatic breathing rather than shallow chest breathing.</li>
        <li><strong>Follow the visual orb:</strong> Match your respiration to the expanding and contracting orb above. As it expands, inhale quietly through your nose. As it holds, remain still without clenching your throat. As it contracts, release air softly through your lips.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintain soft awareness:</strong> Do not strain your lungs. If the count feels too long initially, choose a gentler rhythm or relax your effort. The goal is calmness, not lung capacity testing.</li>
        <li><strong>Transition to rest:</strong> After 5 to 10 minutes of rhythmic pacing, let your breathing return to its natural rhythm, roll over into your favorite sleep position, and let sleep take over.</li>
      </ol>

      <div class="guide-disclaimer">
        <strong>Wellness Notice:</strong> Guided breathing is a gentle relaxation practice. It is not intended as medical treatment for obstructive sleep apnea, cardiovascular conditions, or respiratory disorders. Always breathe comfortably and never force air or strain to hold your breath.
      </div>

      <h3>Frequently Asked Questions</h3>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why does slow breathing make you feel sleepy?</summary>
          <p>Slow breathing with prolonged exhalations activates the parasympathetic nervous system through the vagus nerve. This lowers your heart rate, decreases cortisol (the stress hormone), and prompts the brain to generate soothing alpha brainwaves conducive to sleep onset.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Which breathing pattern is most effective for falling asleep?</summary>
          <p>The 4-7-8 technique is widely considered the most potent for bedtime because its long 8-second exhale strongly stimulates the vagus nerve. However, if holding your breath causes tension, equal-ratio Coherent Breathing (5 in, 5 out) is an exceptional gentle alternative.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How many minutes should I breathe before bed?</summary>
          <p>Between 5 and 10 minutes (approximately 20 to 40 breath cycles) is typically sufficient to shift the nervous system out of an alert state and trigger natural drowsiness.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>What should I do if holding my breath makes me anxious?</summary>
          <p>Some people experience air hunger or mild panic when holding their breath. If this happens, switch to a pattern without breath holds, such as Coherent Breathing or simple 4-second inhale, 6-second exhale rhythms.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Should I breathe through my nose or mouth?</summary>
          <p>Inhaling through your nose is strongly recommended because it warms, humidifies, and filters the air while stimulating nitric oxide production. Exhaling can be done gently through either the nose or pursed lips.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Navigation slot="nav" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    max-width: 32rem;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 1rem 1rem 6rem;
  }

  .page__header {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .page__title {
    font-size: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0.25rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text);
  }

  .page__desc {
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-drift-text-muted);
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
